Polycystic ovary syndrome
Young Adult & Adolecence
Oligomenorrhoea/Amenorrhoea, Clinical hirsutism, Polycystic ovaries on ultrasound

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-032
Reason for Visit: Acute severe psychosocial distress, hyperventilation, and panic episode following the sudden onset of heavy, unexpected vaginal bleeding after six months of amenorrhoea.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a highly distressed patient experiencing a sudden, heavy vaginal bleed and severe cramping after a prolonged period of absent menses, leading to an acute panic attack and fear of a miscarriage or severe pathology. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to rule out an ectopic pregnancy, assess hemodynamic stability, and provide immediate clinical de-escalation prior to morning routine clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I haven't had a period in six months, and suddenly I am bleeding heavily with agonizing cramps, and I am terrified I am losing a pregnancy."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents in a state of acute panic. The underlying chronic history includes secondary amenorrhoea for the past 6 months, alongside progressive clinical hyperandrogenism presenting as moderate hirsutism and refractory acne vulgaris. The patient also notes recent central obesity. The acute event tonight involves the sudden onset of heavy vaginal bleeding, soaking two pads in two hours, accompanied by severe lower abdominal cramping. The unexpected nature of the bleed, combined with baseline mood lability and depressive symptoms, has triggered a severe anxiety episode.
Functional Status: Ambulating, but highly distressed, tearful, and hyperventilating.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute health anxiety; fear of pregnancy loss despite denying recent unprotected intercourse.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 88 Kg
General: Patient is highly anxious, tachypnoeic, crying, and clutching a heating pad to the lower abdomen.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals generalized suprapubic tenderness consistent with severe menstrual cramping; the abdomen is soft with no rigidity, rebound tenderness, or signs of an acute surgical abdomen. Pelvic exam is deferred as bleeding is currently contained with standard sanitary products. Cardiovascular exam shows a sinus tachycardia consistent with acute anxiety and pain. A point-of-care urine beta-hCG test performed immediately during the visit is completely negative. The clinical phenotype strongly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of oligomenorrhoea/amenorrhoea and clinical hirsutism, raising high suspicion for an underlying endocrine disorder that anticipates finding polycystic ovaries on a future ultrasound.
Environmental Observations: Multiple unused sanitary pads on the bathroom counter; patient is actively hyperventilating.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Anovulatory Bleeding secondary to suspected Polycystic Ovary Syndrome (PCOS) - Status: Acute distress
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy / Miscarriage - Ruled out/unlikely due to the definitive negative point-of-care urine beta-hCG test performed during the assessment.

Endometrial Hyperplasia/Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary cause due to the patient's young age, though prolonged unopposed estrogen from anovulation requires formal investigation.

Bleeding Disorder (e.g., von Willebrand disease) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the lack of a lifelong history of easy bruising, epistaxis, or heavy bleeding since menarche.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe acute psychological distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a severe panic episode driven by sudden, heavy vaginal bleeding following prolonged amenorrhoea, immediately fearing a complicated pregnancy loss. The presence of acute tachycardia and heavy bleeding required an urgent after-hours home visit to definitively exclude a life-threatening ectopic pregnancy or hemorrhagic shock. The immediate physical assessment and negative point-of-care pregnancy test instantly de-escalated the acute psychological crisis. Clinical evaluation confirmed hemodynamic stability and identified a classic presentation of anovulatory bleeding secondary to suspected PCOS. Delaying this assessment would have left a highly vulnerable patient in a state of unmanaged panic and potential physical danger if an ectopic pregnancy had been present.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conducted immediate psychological de-escalation using breathing techniques to resolve hyperventilation. Provided definitive clinical reassurance regarding the negative pregnancy test. Administered Ibuprofen 400mg PO stat for the acute cramping. Advised the regular GP to order fasting serology (free androgen index, total testosterone, HbA1c) and a transvaginal pelvic ultrasound to confirm the expected string-of-pearls appearance of subcapsular follicles.
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately manage the severe cramping, take NSAIDs (like Ibuprofen) with food, directly blocking the inflammatory chemicals causing the uterine spasms. To objectively monitor the blood loss and prevent further panic, keep a physical tally of the number of fully soaked pads used every 4 hours, providing concrete data rather than relying on anxious subjective estimation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 7 days to initiate a formal diagnostic workup for PCOS, discuss cyclical progesterone to protect the endometrium, and review the bleeding pattern.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the bleeding becomes so heavy that the patient completely soaks through two heavy-duty sanitary pads per hour for two consecutive hours, or if severe dizziness or fainting occurs upon standing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The current diagnosis is suspected PCOS causing a delayed, heavy period; definitive diagnosis requires blood tests and an ultrasound.
Clinical Course: The heavy bleeding and cramping are typical for a uterine lining that has been building up for six months; it will likely be heavier and longer than a normal period but will eventually taper off.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you begin soaking through more than two thick pads every hour for two hours straight, or if you feel faint and dizzy when you stand up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your hormones have not been cycling normally, your uterine lining has grown very thick over the last six months without shedding. This sudden, heavy bleeding is simply your body finally shedding that thickened lining. It is not a miscarriage, and it is a common symptom of a treatable hormone imbalance.
Actionable Steps: Take your ibuprofen to reduce both the pain and the volume of the bleeding. Keep a strict count of the pads you use to accurately track the bleeding rate.

